\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Clip-level key geometry is set by centroid sample size, not by temporal context. (a) and (b) share a vertical axis, with error bars of one standard deviation over 5 draws in (a) and 10 in (b). (c) The same rise seen as geometry, for the 24 MERT L12 key centroids ordered round the circle of fifths with majors above the white rule. Each panel is rank-transformed over its own entries, which is what a Spearman correlation sees, and each sampled panel is the median of ten draws.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:averaging}{{1}{2}{Clip-level key geometry is set by centroid sample size, not by temporal context. (a) and (b) share a vertical axis, with error bars of one standard deviation over 5 draws in (a) and 10 in (b). (c) The same rise seen as geometry, for the 24 MERT L12 key centroids ordered round the circle of fifths with majors above the white rule. Each panel is rank-transformed over its own entries, which is what a Spearman correlation sees, and each sampled panel is the median of ten draws}{figure.1}{}}
