\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Key information, ambient geometry and readout geometry come apart, on the same 7035 GiantSteps clips and 24 keys. \textit  {acc} is 24-way key accuracy, chance $.042$; $\rho ^{\text  {cent}}_{5}$ is the circle-of-fifths correlation among the 24 key centroids and $\rho ^{W}_{5}$ the same among the probe's class weight vectors; $\rho _{\text  {chr}}$ is semitone adjacency, a discriminant a generic tendency for nearby keys to look alike would also raise. Partialling out mel-spectral centroid distance changes no sign. Orthogonal key codes decode at $.651$ with classes equidistant by construction and still show nothing, so $\rho ^{W}_{5}$ is not an artefact of an accurate classifier. The four values in bold are the mirror-image pair of Sec.~\ref {sec:three}.}}{3}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:decodable-vs-geometric}{{1}{3}{Key information, ambient geometry and readout geometry come apart, on the same 7035 GiantSteps clips and 24 keys. \textit {acc} is 24-way key accuracy, chance $.042$; $\rho ^{\text {cent}}_{5}$ is the circle-of-fifths correlation among the 24 key centroids and $\rho ^{W}_{5}$ the same among the probe's class weight vectors; $\rho _{\text {chr}}$ is semitone adjacency, a discriminant a generic tendency for nearby keys to look alike would also raise. Partialling out mel-spectral centroid distance changes no sign. Orthogonal key codes decode at $.651$ with classes equidistant by construction and still show nothing, so $\rho ^{W}_{5}$ is not an artefact of an accurate classifier. The four values in bold are the mirror-image pair of Sec.~\ref {sec:three}}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Two operations on NSynth notes, with one outcome each. (a) and (b) share a vertical axis, since rescaling (a) would invent structure that is not there. (a) Averaging notes of the same pitch into a pitch centroid does not produce octave equivalence at any sample size; the chromagram is off scale, moving only from 0.57 to 0.62. (b) A rank-4 projection fitted only on GiantSteps track tonics, applied unchanged to individual recordings, with instrument- bootstrap 95\% intervals. (c) The curve (b) summarises, in the same colours.}}{3}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mechanisms}{{2}{3}{Two operations on NSynth notes, with one outcome each. (a) and (b) share a vertical axis, since rescaling (a) would invent structure that is not there. (a) Averaging notes of the same pitch into a pitch centroid does not produce octave equivalence at any sample size; the chromagram is off scale, moving only from 0.57 to 0.62. (b) A rank-4 projection fitted only on GiantSteps track tonics, applied unchanged to individual recordings, with instrument- bootstrap 95\% intervals. (c) The curve (b) summarises, in the same colours}{figure.2}{}}
